{
  "id": "remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array-ii",
  "questionId": "remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array-ii",
  "slug": "remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array-ii",
  "title": "Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "two pointers",
    "in-place filtering"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "since the array is already sorted, all copies of the same value are grouped together - you just need to cap each group at 2",
    "keep a write pointer for where the next kept element goes; a new element is safe to keep unless it equals the element already sitting two spots before the write pointer",
    "why two spots back specifically? because if nums[i] equals the value at write pointer - 2, that means the last two kept slots are already that same value, so a third would be one too many",
    "start the write pointer at index 2 for arrays with at least 2 elements, since the first two elements are always safe to keep as-is",
    "walk through by hand on [1,1,1,2,2,3] and track the write pointer to see exactly when it skips versus advances"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "comparing against the immediately preceding kept element instead of the one two positions back, which only enforces 'no more than 1 duplicate' instead of 2",
    "assuming the input is unsorted and trying to count occurrences globally, which is unnecessary extra work given the sorted precondition",
    "off-by-one when initializing the write pointer, causing the first two elements to be mishandled",
    "confusing this with the plain 'remove duplicates' problem, which only allows 1 copy of each value instead of 2"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of integers already sorted in non-decreasing order.\n\n**Trim it down so that every distinct value appears at most twice, while keeping the remaining elements in their original (sorted) relative order, and return the trimmed list.**\n\nThe classic in-place version of this problem cares about not using extra space and reporting a count, but for this exercise just return the resulting list directly.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 3 * 10^4",
      "-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4",
      "nums is sorted in non-decreasing order"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "every element is the same value (result keeps only the first two)",
      "no element repeats at all (result equals the input)",
      "a single-element array",
      "a value that appears exactly three or four times in a row"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1,1,1,2,2,3]",
        "output": "[1,1,2,2,3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1,1,2,2,3]",
        "explanation": "the third 1 is dropped since 1 already appears twice; everything else appears at most twice already"
      },
